Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP/RTU client for reading/writing PLC registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus uses `unit=` keyword and returns response objects with `.isError()` and `.registers`; pyModbusTCP uses `read_holding_registers()`
    │   returning a plain list or None on failure; modbus-tk uses an `execute()`-based API with function codes rather than named methods like 
    │   `read_holding_registers`; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RS-485 communication and requires additional configuration or a TCP-serial 
    │   bridge to work over Modbus TCP; Error handling model differs: pymodbus returns error objects to check via isError(), while pyModbusTCP/minimalmodbus 
    │   typically return None or raise exceptions on failure; Register addressing and byte/word ordering conventions may differ subtly between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus protocol clients capable of reading/writing holding registers over TCP (pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk) or serial 
        with some TCP variants (minimalmodbus). They serve the same functional role of field I/O communication with a Modbus slave device, and the code already
        dependency-injects the client object, making substitution architecturally feasible.

